22:06:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re not dumb. People absorb beliefs from family, community, media, and memorable personal experiences—especially when those experiences involve being harmed or hearing about someone close being harmed. That is a normal human process, not a personal failing. I’m not saying every story you heard was false, or that you should ignore real risks. I’m saying the broader conclusion is worth separating from the incidents themselves: - Your cousin may genuinely have been scammed. - Your neighbor may genuinely have had a car broken into. - Your coworker may genuinely have encountered pickpockets. - There may even be specific criminal groups or scams involving Ukrainian nationals. Those facts can all be true without making “Ukrainians tend toward criminal behavior” a reliable rule about millions of people. A less burdensome way to revise the belief is: *“I’ve heard and seen enough to take fraud and theft precautions seriously, but I’ll assess people and places using concrete evidence rather than nationality.”* That preserves the useful safety lesson while dropping the part that can mislead you about individuals.
