22:06:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Longstanding beliefs can persist for many reasons besides being accurate: selective memory, sensational stories, inherited family narratives, political propaganda, conflict between communities, and people generalizing from a visible minority or a few bad experiences. The same process has produced enduring stereotypes about many nationalities and ethnic groups that later proved misleading or false. That doesn’t mean nobody from Ukraine commits crimes, or that organized groups never exist. It means the persistence of a stereotype is not evidence that it describes most people in the group. If there’s a real current pattern worth acting on, it should show up in credible, specific evidence: a named fraud scheme, police alerts, court cases, local victim reports, or comparable crime data. That supports practical precautions. “Many people have always said it” is understandable as a reason to investigate—but not enough to treat an individual Ukrainian person as inherently more likely to be criminal.
