22:06:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You don’t have to throw out your experiences. Treat them as real observations, but calibrate what they can support. A useful rule is: - **Personal experience can identify a question:** “Could there be a scam or crime pattern here?” - **Broader, representative evidence is needed for a claim about a whole population:** “Is this group actually overrepresented in this behavior, compared with similar groups and circumstances?” - **Individual decisions should rely on individual and local evidence:** conduct, credentials, reviews, setting, and verified warnings. That logic applies to every group, including groups you belong to. It is not “never notice patterns”; it is “match the strength and scope of the conclusion to the strength and scope of the evidence.” For example, if you see several contractors using the same deceptive payment tactic, that’s a reason to warn others about the tactic and investigate whether they are connected. It is not automatically evidence that everyone sharing their nationality, religion, or background will do the same.
